On 2007-11-15, Kent Paul Dolan <xanthian@well.com> wrote:
> Rast <ra...@yahoo.com> wrote:
>
>> There isn't any legitimate reason for wanting
>> others to be unable to watch your replays.
>
> Here are several:

I mostly agree, but I have a couple of nitpicks:

> * I have trade secrets I don't care to share with
>   people competing with me on the score table.

I think we all take advantage of each other's experiences, both here on
RGRN and on public servers like nethack.alt.org. I can't think of any
players (though there must be some, of course) who wouldn't be proud
to have their good ideas used by everyone else. If you're really that
awesome a player, you'll come up with new trade secrets to shock everyone
with next year. :)

> * I don't want others to know how good a bones file
>   I just dropped, when I might be able to get to it
>   first.

Your chances of getting it first are minuscule on a server where forty
people are playing at the same time. (This didn't stop me from trying,
the once, when I had choked on a wraith corpse on DL9.)

> * Signing up to play on a public server doesn't
>   mean I care to have my game play blunders being
>   subject to public ridicule.

Yup, fair, though if you're killed by a blue jelly and it's in the
logfile, there's not much you can do about that. :)

> * Signing up to play on a public server is not a
>   relinquishment of personal privacy.

It does mean that your end result and whatever pseudonym you choose are
going to be in the logfile, though that's significantly less information
than ttyrecs or even dumplogs. 

> * I don't approve of snooping, and my already
>   rampant paranoia makes me get twitchy when I feel
>   like people are watching over my shoulder, whether
>   in real time or otherwise.

Arguably a personal problem, but I can empathize. :)

> * Signing up to a public server isn't an instance
>   of volunteering to be an instructor or to create
>   instructional materials. The implied agenda for
>   signing on to play NetHack on a public server ends
>   at "play the game NetHack".

With devnull, there's also an implied "compete" with most people,
though that's still not instructional.

> Many more could be written, those should suffice.

Even with those minor objections, these are all perfectly valid reasons
not to play on a public server.  I don't have a problem with public
servers choosing to archive ttyrecs, and people with your reasons not
playing on those public servers.

I think enough people playing in devnull would _want_ this functionality
that having it there would be a great idea; whether reasons for wanting
replays hidden are legitimate or not, it's easy enough to make people
like you happy by offering an option to turn them off (I assume), so it's
probably a good idea for devnull. I'm not worried about this, because
I don't think anyone I would want to watch would turn theirs off :)

I have an existing strong opinion, as I run a public server, and I record
and broadcast all games; I realize and accept that this probably loses me
people. I'm more interested in fostering a community of people interested
in sharing the game with each other, which I think has happened, and is
awesome. Devnull has different goals and might not want to lose those
people, but unless those people are a much larger group than services like
NAO would suggest, I think defaulting to recording games is sane. Last
year, a bunch of us worked to make a client-side infrastructure for
broadcasting games because it was the feature we most wanted; there are
a number of people out there who very much _do_ want the feature.

-r.


